The graphs and automation are fantastic.

My quick read is that NUTS is

* a bit faster for sampling beta[1] (intercept, perhaps?)

* a bit faster for sampling beta[2] and beta[3] with
  fewer than 32 times as many data points as parameters

* a bit slower for sampling beta[2] and beta[3] with
  more than 32 times as many data points as parameters

* much slower at sampling tau.

Is this the non-centered (Matt-trick) parameterization?
I'm assuming so because of the raw parameters.

How are you generating the predictors x?

Should all those x axes be labeled n_eff/s?  Warmup
time or RAM don't make sense that way.

I also didn't understand what neffpers meant.

How many ensemble members were you using?  2K for K
parameters?  (I think it should be whatever emcee's
default is, but their doc says that if you're having
problem fitting, increasing the number of ensemble members
can help.)

Some more questions inline.

On Jul 23, 2014, at 2:58 PM, Peter Li <li.pe...@gmail.com> wrote:

