From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Sat Jul 26 21:56:45 2014
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Sat, 26 Jul 2014 21:56:45 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] NUTS properties in the convex polytope problem
Message-ID: <Sc5PwZaE8fQ.v8iL5VwhQz8J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Sc5PwZaE8fQ.Gsue505VfMcJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <Sc5PwZaE8fQ.Gsue505VfMcJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/Sc5PwZaE8fQ/m/v8iL5VwhQz8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> To review, the problem is given fixed matrix A and vector b, sample x uniformally subject to the constraints that x  > 0 and Ax = b. So, there are an infinite number of solutions to that system of linear equations and they are interested in the strictly positive subset. From Wikipiedia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_of_linear_equations#Matrix_solution>, we see that all the (unconstrained) solutions for x
>
>
>
> can be expressed in terms of the unrestricted vector w and the Moore-Penrose inverse of A, denoted A^g. Then, Stan could parameterize the problem in terms of w and decide whether to reject based on x.

Okay, let’s think about this geometrically.  Every linearly-independent column in A gives a constraint of the form

\sum_{j} A_{ij} x_{j} = b_{i}

or

\sum_{j} A_{ij} x_{j} / b_{i} = 1.

If the x_{j}, the A_{ij}, and the b_{i} are all positive then this defines a scaled simplex.

So, again assuming that everything is positive, every linearly-independent column in A defines a simplex of possible solutions.  When the intersection of all of these simplices is nonempty we have a solution.  For example, if dim(x) = 3 and A has two linearly-independent columns then any solutions lie on a line segment where the two triangles intersect.  If A has three linearly-independent columns then we get another triangle, and the intersection of all three is a point representing the single solution.

How, what is the equation above doing?  Again, think about it column by column.  If A has only one column with elements A_{i} then the equation becomes

x_{i} = A_{i} b + (I_{ij} - A_{i} A_{j} / (sum_{k} A_{k}^{2} ) ) w_{j}.

But this is exactly a projection operator!  (I_{ij} - A_{i} A_{j} / (sum_{k} A_{k}^{2} ) ) projects w onto the hyperplane on which the simplex lives and A_{i} b defines the offset of that plane from zero.  This means that provided a solution exists, any w is guaranteed to be projected onto the hyperplane that contains the solution manifold.  We then just have to reject if the point isn’t within the solution manifold itself.

Basically the strategy of sampling the w and then rejecting any resulting x that are negative is like sampling using our existing simplex transform but ignoring the Jacobian.  Without the Jacobian to concentrate the probability, the chain on the unconstrained w space will wander and look terrible but the projection back onto the constrained solution space won’t look that bad because the wander gets concentrated onto a compact space.

> But according to the OP, the results are better than any other MCMC algorithm that he has tried for this problem.

Well, if he was just using RWM or a Gibbs sampler then, yeah, this would do far better.  I doubt he was using a slice sampler or anything sufficiently adaptive to mimic HMC, so there’s no contradiction in your intuition.

If the target distribution on the unconstrained w space is uniform then all samplers will random walk.  HMC just random walks faster than everything else.

> There is one color for each of the eight chains (i.e. they don't even converge) and I had to zoom way in because there is the occasional huge outlier that (I think implies the moments do not exist and) would obscure the prism-like shape of the sample space for w (at least in this specific case where A = [1, 1, 1] and b = 1). Also, within each chain, the margins of w are multimodal

I think you’re being fooled by random walks, which will wander around making wacky s-like paths that project down to multiple modes.

> In conclusion, given that the draws of x seem to be more than adequate for this particular A and b when my understanding of the NUTS theory would suggest that they should be less than adequate, how optimistic should we be about NUTS for a general A and b.?Or even more generally for the case of a dodgy unconstrained space when only the constrained space is of interest? Or even more generally when no strong form of ergodicity holds, but we have many chains that all seem to have the same distribution (at least for x) in a finite number of iterations?

The biggest issue here is whether or not the system has a solution — if it does then the above strategy will have similar “success” for general A, b, and x with everything positive.

I scare-quote “success” because even though the target distribution should be easy to sample from (uniform distribution on a compact space) you’re making it harder than it should be by mapping to a non-compact space, changing the target distribution, and destroying all of the nice ergodicity guarantees.  Because you’re projecting onto a compact space it will just be harder to see the failures.

But if everything is positive and we know a solution exists then just model the constrained space directly!  For example, in the above case of A = (1, 1, 1) and b=1 you’re just sampling uniformly from the simplex and all you need is

parameters {
  simplex[n] x;
}

model {
  \\ nothing — maybe a Dirichlet prior if you wanted to be cool
}

In the general case of A_{i}, b positive you just define a transformed parameter,

parameters {
  simplex[n] x_tilde;
}

transformed parameters {
  vector[n] x;
  for (i in 1:n)
    x[i] <- b * x[i] / A[i];

model {
  \\ nothing — maybe a Dirichlet prior if you wanted to be cool
}

