On Jul 29, 2014, at 3:00 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:

>> By all means a different branch!  I'm sure Mitzi can handle
>> the JSON integration.
>
> It is actually easy to incorporate more var_context implementations,
> but we should figure out the basic structure first (in particular, the
> implementations should really be moved out into the interfaces).

If we want to implement a JSON read from a file (or arbitrary input
stream), that can be done once and for all in our C++.  If you want to use
a JSON lib from R or Python, then it'd have to be in the interfaces.
If we want to have users read data into R or Python first,
then just use the from memory var_context, then that'd go in
the interfaces, and then we don't even need to include JSON readers
as dependencies.

For me, it's all about reducing code redundancy and package
dependencies.

>> By user facing, I meant to ask if they're the codes returned
>> by the command process to the OS.  By all means that should
>> be as informative as possible, and also follow the standard
>> conventions for errors (I think 0 is the non-error return).
>
> That's an interface issue, and sort of implemented in command.hpp
> which cmdstan calls.  Dan used standard values (see the doc) but
> they aren't quite flexible enough to cover things that initialization
> failure.

There aren't any standard values from the OS perspective, other
than that I think 0 is used for normal returns almost everywhere.
See, e.g.,

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exit_status

The main concern is setting things up so the calling process can
do the right thing with the return.  For standard command-line usage,
this is less relevant and the error messages that get printed are
how to pass information to users.

>> With base classes, you have to be careful not to slice anywhere --- everything has
>> to be done by reference or pointer (I'd VERY STRONGLY prefer references everywhere,
>> as they're always guaranteed to be defined, unlike pointers).
>
> In the implementation these classes are templated out and passed by preference
> or constructed in the function itself.
>
> I just want a base class so we can tell interface devs "Wanna write an interface?  Just implement
> these abstract base classes and you're 99% of the way there".

I understand.  And it's easier to do it with a base clas,
because concepts can't be expressed in the C++ object language.

And as long as everything's passed by reference whenever assigned,
then there's no problem using base classes.

- Bob

